{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus","id":1477060,"title":"Narlthus","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-03-23T01:07:57-07:00","id":1477060,"title":"Narlthus","content":"Narlthus was introduced very early in the game in the 1e sourcebook Rite of Passage. It was heavily tied to North America as the Wendigo Theurge She-Cries-Ice was the Garou that bound the bane (IMO she should be a Legendry Garou and awesome Ancestor spirit). Nevertheless, Narlthus was mentioned as a Talon of the Wyrm that meant it was intrinsically involved with the myths of the entire Garou Nation, not just the Three Brothers. In my own chronicles, I have the Eurasian tribes recognize Narlthus as a major threat, but ignorant of how it was defeated until they finally made contact with the Three Brothers (some 10,000 to 12,000 years later!).<br /> <br /> I am working on creating various &quot;factions&quot; of the Wyrm to provide the game more depth in my chronicles. So I associate corrupt winter spirits with Narltus and the false Wendigo (of Ways of the Wolf sourcebook) and other bane spirits that make sense.<br /> <br /> As inspiration, I use a lot of the Ithaqua mythos of August Derleth (prominent in the Call of Cthulhu RPG) and similar media. I want the spirit to be more mysterious to Eurasian Garou than the Three Brothers, but I make some reference to it to Fimbul Winter and such so that certain Old World tribes understand the Bane in its own way.<br /> <br /> Interested in what other people have done with this Bane in their chronicles, or what potential they think the Bane has. While as presented in Rite of Passage, the Bane/Incarna is a revelation to modern Garou, I prefer to use it as an ongoing threat that just became more relevant with the events of Rite of Passage. What do you think?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-27T19:16:03-07:00","id":1477559,"title":"","content":"I take the complete lack of new posts to mean that essentially people haven't done anything with Narlthus in their chronicles!<br /> <br /> While not an entirely unexpected outcome, it is surprising in the sense that Rite of Passage was one of the original WW adventures and meant to start PCs from the original setting of the Sept of the Green. The assumption would be that many gaming groups utilized this setting and explored the the game from there. And while Rite of Passage gave us NPCs who thought Narlthus was a Talon of the Wyrm, the book itself stated it could have been any number of things. It was left to the ST. It was primarily meant as a plot hook and plot device that STs could expand into whatever they needed. If players actually haven't done this, it means the New York setting of Werewolf didn't hook players as well as Chicago By Night (and its associated adventrues) did for VtM players.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-30T23:08:45-07:00","id":1477752,"title":"","content":"In every game involved Narlthus my players stoped the enemy from releasing the bane. I was too young runing the game and I din't used him as I would now, for example by influencing reality. The same problem touches Malefaxus.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1784-werewolf43","name":"werewolf43","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1784_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"315"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-04T11:43:43-07:00","id":1478013,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>werewolf43</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus?p=1477752#post1477752\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In every game involved Narlthus my players stoped the enemy from releasing the bane.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's probably the end result of most games. Even though Rite of Passage describes what would happen if it was actually released, I wonder how many times this was actually done in game. Most STs don't want the PCs to fail, or figure out the impact of a big setting changing event. Though I'm sure it's happened a few times.<br /> <br /> I am actually less interested in the use of Narlthus itself as an antagonist against PCs or Garou characters, and more as a &quot;faction&quot;. By that I mean, what are other kinds of NPCs (human cultists, occult researchers, Banes, corrupted spirits, fomori, other monsters) I can use to create stories for PCs. I can only do so much with Narlthus itself as an entity. But if there are other characters involved with it, I can do multiple stories. Even with Narlthus being imprisoned, I can get a lot of mileage out of that without even needing to specifically run the Rite of Passage adventure.<br /> <br /> For example, in one of my Vampire games, a player wanted to play a Gangrel heavily inspired by real world wendigo lore. Mechanically, I just told him to use the Curse flaw, and we came up with the specifics of the curse to reflect the wendigo lore he wanted to include. But to explain the curse, I tied it to Narlthus and how some of the first (Sabbat) Gangrel to arrive in the New World encountered the mound (sometime in the late 1500s), sensed occult power there, and was corrupted by it. Those tainted Sabbat Gangrel now pass the curse of the wendigo to their childer. (The PC did not know anything about this as he was an abandoned childe, but if he ever tried to hunt down his origins, he could learn this.) <br /> <br /> Once I did that, I ported it over to my Werewolf chronicles since they're a good foe for the actual Wendigo tribe of Garou. There aren't many of these vampire &quot;wendigo&quot;, but now I have a nice little foe tied to Narlthus that allows me to run more plots related to this foe. (Now that I think about this having reread RoP, it would make perfect narrative sense for these to be the five Sabbat Gangrel bodyguards for Carmen, the Tremere anti-tribu helping Azarea excavate Narlthus's prison.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-11T20:41:54-07:00","id":1478640,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus#post1477060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interested in what other people have done with this Bane in their chronicles, or what potential they think the Bane has. While as presented in Rite of Passage, the Bane/Incarna is a revelation to modern Garou, I prefer to use it as an ongoing threat that just became more relevant with the events of Rite of Passage. What do you think?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>My friend used to run this story. Twice. First time we used pregenerated characters. They spend some time with Billy, then were trapped by the snowstorm at Kroder's Pass. Later they were found by Black Spiral Dancers sent by Azaera. BSD convinced them that Wendigo from the sept lied to them when they said that the pack can go unmolested instead sending the Icedagger pack after them. BSD blindfolded the characters (for their own protection) and then send them to Malfeas. Unfortunately none of the pregens had Sense Wyrm or Truth of Gaia (which would spoil the BSD plan), only Scent the True Form (which allowed the characters to identify BSD as Garou).<br /> Second time we used characters we created. After we were exiled from the sept we did the only reasonable thing we could: go back to NY ASAP (first of all it's typical for Bone Gnawers to send their cubs to a distant sept and see how fast they can return; second there was a murder mystery to solve). He even tried to stop Azaera giving two players from his other group the only characters who knew at least some of her plan (Accolon, homid Ahroun Stargazer, Elder who went there following his vision and Rolling Stone, homid Ragabash Silent Strider, cliath send by Sept of the Green to find out why Wendigo don't respond; Acollon was described in Rite of Passage, Rolling Stone was his creation, mentioned in RoP) and let them played them after our characters left. Acollon died gloriously trying to stop Azaera, Rolling Stone returned with the news (Acollon's order, in case he didn't make it).<br /> After that he run a campaign during which Azaera tried to find other Wyrm Talons and free Narlthus and the rest of the world tried to stop her. First she discredited both Wendigo sept and Sept of the Green using her agents as unable to stop her. After the political turmoil ended she suggested that one of the Talons is hidden in the Amazon (as no Theurge know how many of Talons there are) sending us in a wild goose chase, then to Russia for Koshchei and used the plot of Werewolf: the Apocalypse Heart of Gaia unreleased video game (basing on the cutscenes which he found on Youtube; I later did the same). Unfortunately I can't tell you how it ended because he died before he could do that. But from what I know about him it would propably end either during a heroic (and tragic) battle with Azaera after which Pentex would hide the Talons (like the ark in &quot;Indiana Jones&quot;) or Billy would return and let PCs use his Horn of Fenris thus summon ancestor spirits of the First Pack for an epic battle with Talons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-19T12:39:34-07:00","id":1479147,"title":"","content":"I never ran or played Rite of Passage, but I was inspired enough a few years ago to try and account for all four (or five) of the Talons of the Wyrm and made a write-up <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/0By9UuUWWH6CiZlA0al9XdkIybXM/edit?usp=sharing&amp;ouid=117821193628514848482&amp;resourcekey=0-N6OUOc3-4N2QXMYBYN6HNQ&amp;rtpof=true&amp;sd=true\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>. Three Talons of the Wyrm have been mentioned by name in the W:tA books: Narlthus (assumed to be the &quot;Thumb&quot; Talon) in <i>Rite of Passage</i>, Koshchei (the &quot;First Finger&quot; or &quot;He-Who-Points&quot;) in <i>Rage Across Russia</i> and Thrassus Thrice-Damned (the &quot;Little Finger) in <i>Book of the Weaver</i>. <br /> <br /> No one knows for certain whether there were four or five Talons of the Wyrm, but assuming there were five, I wrote up two more short blurbs for 1) Galchus (the &quot;Middle Finger&quot; or &quot;He-Who-Scorns&quot;) who was imprisoned by the Uktena and Wendigo into an artifact (actually, an artifact <i>within</i> another artifact; see the write-up link for details) that was buried underneath the Arctic Ocean, and for 2) Vothnaak, (the &quot;Ring Finger&quot;/Fourth Talon of the Wyrm), who was imprisoned in an artifact that somehow wound up in the Womb, the vault of forbidden items at the Sept of the Bygone Visions (mentioned in <i>Caerns: Places of Power</i>). My notes are pretty raw, admittedly, but perhaps if you guys are curious about the Talons of the Wyrm, maybe my notes will inspire you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/835/7xu8.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/folderview?id=0By9UuUWWH6CiMVlhWHMwNWcyaG8&amp;usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Complete Garou Gifts List, Fera Gifts List, Garou Totems List and other assorted OPP/White Wolf goodies</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2710-su-tehp","name":"Su-tehp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2710_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"532"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-19T13:55:37-07:00","id":1479149,"title":"","content":"The Talons of the Wyrm was an idea introduced very early on, but was severely neglected afterwards. I wonder if this was something meant to be something much bigger in Werewolf lore and setting, but after Bill Bridges left as Developer, whatever plans he had for them was forgotten or neglected. I don't remember them much in the later work when Ethan Skemp was Developer, not even in the final Apocalypse book. He had other concepts he was interested in. We're certainly given very little information about them other than their release would be terrible, and therefore it's a matter of stopping whatever immediate steps are being done by someone else to release them.<br /> <br /> Koschei probably has the most information about him as he has several pages about him in Rage Across Russia. Unfortunately it's not very evocative. He's not described well except that once grown to full power he'll free the other Talons and a lot of other imprisoned Banes. He's just a plot device to be defeated once the heroes collect enough plot coupons. There's no personality. No theme. No evocative legends. By comparison, Baba Yaga is a far more interesting character and faction.<br /> <br /> We have even less about Exhibit 338, but since it's blatantly a rip off from John Carpenter's Prince of Darkness, we can easily get inspiration by watching the movie (unfortunately, while there's some neat things about it, I don't think it's a particularly good movie.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-25T23:51:47-07:00","id":1479535,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus?p=1479149#post1479149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Talons of the Wyrm was an idea introduced very early on, but was severely neglected afterwards. I wonder if this was something meant to be something much bigger in Werewolf lore and setting, but after Bill Bridges left as Developer, whatever plans he had for them was forgotten or neglected. I don't remember them much in the later work when Ethan Skemp was Developer, not even in the final Apocalypse book. He had other concepts he was interested in. We're certainly given very little information about them other than their release would be terrible, and therefore it's a matter of stopping whatever immediate steps are being done by someone else to release them.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is, pretty debatable imho, to be fair. While later books - Rage Across Russia mainly and Book of the Wyrm i think - speak of the Talons as an actual, but never clearly defined, fact and entity, things are <b>considerably </b>more dubious and elusive if one reads Rite of Passage on its own. Half the point of Narlthus is that <b><i>no one really knows</i></b> what it really is, all the theories given for its mysterious power/aura of evil (talon of the wyrm, demon, insane corrupt fae, alien eldritch horror and more) could be <i>completely f##king wrong</i> and part of the point is that, claims of deep lore and knowledge notwithstanding, the high-ranked allies and antagonists involved <b><i>are just as in the dark as the Rank 1 cubs</i></b> trying to stop its awakening/liberation.<br /> <br /> Damn, at some point in the adventure it's mentioned Carmen, the Tremere antitribu that is a major ally in advancing Azaera's crazy scheme, <i><b>believes Narlthus to be an antediluvian</b></i> - that Rite of Passage gives us <b>no explanation whatsoever</b> for what kind of insane troll logic could led someone to such an idea - or allying with a BSD trying to awake it for that matter, is ever given to the reader is the adventure's one major failing, in my humble opinion. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> As an aside, i came across <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Carmen\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> while looking into the adventure today, what could bring even more questions &amp; ideas about her and <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Toronto_(WOD)\" target=\"_blank\">possible uses</a> to one's game.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus?p=1479149#post1479149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Koschei probably has the most information about him as he has several pages about him in Rage Across Russia. Unfortunately it's not very evocative. He's not described well except that once grown to full power he'll free the other Talons and a lot of other imprisoned Banes. He's just a plot device to be defeated once the heroes collect enough plot coupons. There's no personality. No theme. No evocative legends. By comparison, Baba Yaga is a far more interesting character and faction.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, i'd say that is partly because Baba Yaga is <i>supposed to be</i> Rage Across Russia's final boss, while Koschei is the big bad MacGuffin that makes even her helping a pack of heroic PCs kind of believable. Also, i suspect they simply counted in people looking into <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koschei\" target=\"_blank\">the sources</a> of the character for inspiration. But then hearing actual russian folktales as a kid from my now long departed grandma is pretty much the core reason i <i><b>never ever</b> </i>STed that book's plot and cast as written, so one's milleage may vary. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus?p=1479149#post1479149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We have even less about Exhibit 338, but since it's blatantly a rip off from John Carpenter's Prince of Darkness, we can easily get inspiration by watching the movie (unfortunately, while there's some neat things about it, I don't think it's a particularly good movie.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, i remember that one. In fact i have toyed with the idea of making a &quot;Gehenna arc&quot; out of Carpenter's <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince_of_Darkness_(film)\" target=\"_blank\">Apocalypse Trilogy</a> for years, but never really went through with it, unfortunately.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 12-04-2022, 12:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-26T12:41:09-07:00","id":1479556,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus?p=1479535#post1479535\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">things are <b>considerably </b>more dubious and elusive if one reads Rite of Passage on its own. Half the ponit of Narlthus is that <b><i>no one really knows</i></b> what it really is, all the theories given for its mysterious power/aura of evil (talon of the wyrm, demon, insane corrupt fae, alien eldritch horror and more) could be <i>completely f##king wrong</i> and part of the point is that, claims of deep lore and knowledge notwithstanding, the high-ranked allies and antagonists involved <b><i>are just as in the dark as the Rank 1 cubs</i></b> trying to stop its awakening/liberation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep, that's completely true. The book itself allows the ST to determine what precisely Narlthus is. Nevertheless, the later books definitely act as if Narlthus' status as a Talon of the Wyrm is the canonical take. That's easily changed by any ST because Narlthus itself never becomes part of an adventure again so it needn't disrupt any chronicle's pre-existing status.<br /> <br /> And since the nature of the Talons of the Wyrm was never really explained except as &quot;big, bad, evil&quot;, in many chronicles it'd be indifferent as to whether Narlthus is a Talon or just something else.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus?p=1479535#post1479535\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Damn, at some point in the adventure it's mentioned Carmen, the Tremere antitribu that is a major ally in advancing Azaera's crazy scheme, <i><b>believes Narlthus to be an antediluvian</b></i> - that Rite of Passage gives us <b>no explanation whatsoever</b> for what kind of insane troll logic could led someone to such an idea - or allying with a BSD trying to awake it for that matter, is ever given to the reader is the adventure's one major failing, in my humble opinion. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. It is a strange leap of logic. However, vampires often tend to reduce the big mysteries to things that they understand - their own vampire lore and history. An &quot;ancient, lost thing of evil&quot; = &quot;antedeluvian&quot; is a leap to a conclusion, but makes sense on the surface.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-26T17:33:53-07:00","id":1479564,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus?p=1479556#post1479556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yep, that's completely true. The book itself allows the ST to determine what precisely Narlthus is. Nevertheless, the later books definitely act as if Narlthus' status as a Talon of the Wyrm is the canonical take. That's easily changed by any ST because Narlthus itself never becomes part of an adventure again so it needn't disrupt any chronicle's pre-existing status.<br /> <br /> And since the nature of the Talons of the Wyrm was never really explained except as &quot;big, bad, evil&quot;, in many chronicles it'd be indifferent as to whether Narlthus is a Talon or just something else.</div> </div> </div> </div>The <b>title </b>&quot;Talon of the Wyrm&quot; is definitely a thing within the Garou Nation's mythos the way canon developed - my point is that <i>as the piece of mythology it is</i>, how much relevance it has beyond a label for any &quot;off the charts sealed evil in a can&quot; that comes up in a pack or sept's way, as either legendry or a resurfacing threat to be stopped.<br /> <br /> - Maybe they are true physical part's of the original Wyrm's essence in the material world.<br /> - Maybe it's just a random collection of top tier wyrm spirits changed from time to time by Garou leadership like the Cam's Red List as lurking dooms reappear, disappear or &quot;newer&quot; even greater ones crop up.<br /> - Maybe some are not of the Wyrm, spirits or even neither of those things, but got the label anyway due to power and antagonism.<br /> <br /> It can be taken at face value or just be a bit of irrelevant nonsense of no actual mystical meaning spawned by the Garou's rampant propensity for mythologizing and storytelling, in the end it's all up to the individual ST (though i defenitely prefer the latter, due to Talons vagueness as actual entities beyond OP bogeymen).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus?p=1479556#post1479556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah. It is a strange leap of logic. However, vampires often tend to reduce the big mysteries to things that they understand - their own vampire lore and history. An &quot;ancient, lost thing of evil&quot; = &quot;antedeluvian&quot; is a leap to a conclusion, but makes sense on the surface.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Honestly, i always found that to be more of a handwavium when inserting vampires in crossover games. If anything the kindred are the first to admit they do <b>not </b>know everything weird in the world and their mythology's does not even try to pretend at having all answers.<br /> <br /> <br /> But anyway, since my last post yesterday, i decided to look into Rites of Passage to see if i could come up with a rationale of sorts for Carmen's belief in a connection of Narlthus to the Caine Mythos and how. Hold my Blood Mary.<br /> <br /> The book mentions that Narlthus' release would be felt as &quot;a great evil's liberation&quot; by every Black Spiral Dancer, vampire and bane (in that order) within a 1000 miles radius. Hundreds of banes will be consumed, as well as all the blood pool of any vampires present.<br /> <br /> This sentence could be easily chalked up as &quot;OP big bad gimmick&quot; but at the same time there are some quite relevant specifics.<br /> <br /> - Black Spiral Dancers, vampires and banes - <b>not </b>Garou, pure or wyrm-tainted (relevant since there is at least one wyrm-tainted Garou in the adventure itself) or other Fera (idem, since werebear is a major NPC), non-wyrm spirits or other supernaturals. Those three particular classes of being apparently have a special rapport/ressonance with it.<br /> <br /> But then we get back to the twist: &quot;Hundreds of banes <b>will be consumed</b>, as well as <b>all the blood pool of any vampires present</b>.&quot;<br /> <br /> For one, that little stunt of Narlthus ressonates <b>a lot</b> with some imagery we have seen from V20 onwards on Antediluvians and their messing with the blood of their childer and/or those bound to them. For another, it makes imprisoned Narlthus into the Stone That Drinks Blood, a most peculiar twisted mirror/opposite to the Stone That Weeps Blood worshipped by the Lost Tribe and that is supposedly what remains of Zillah.<br /> <br /> As an aside, the fact this particular capacity of Narlthus is both quite broad &quot;<b>all </b>the blood pool of <b>any</b> vampires&quot; - read, clan makes no difference - and yet limited &quot;of any vampires <b>present</b>&quot; in radius kinds of resonates with a &quot;heir of the 2nd generation&quot; vibe imho.<br /> <br /> <br /> \u200bOh yeah, the possiblity of finding an entity similar and potentially related to the pillar of all Lost Tribe legendry is <b>definitely </b>something for a Black Hand member to go completely bonkers over. Damn, that kind of correlation is potential Gehenna Crusade fuel level..<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> (Seriously, Polonia's crusade in the turn of the millenium was spurred with much less than this)<br /> <br /> <br /> But wait, wait, there's more, it gets even better.<br /> <br /> If Vitae = Banes, would that mean that feeding DRAWS OUT BANES from within humans and DIGESTS THEM into Vitae?<br /> <br /> The Beast is a sort of incipient bane-like urge resulting of all the processed banestuff flowing through undead flesh and decreasing Humanity/Path while not directly related to serving the Wyrm, does reflect a slow ascension to dominance of the coagulated dark urges that compose the Vitae.<br /> <br /> That would give a boatload of extra layers of meaning to Garou calling vampires &quot;leeches&quot; - and suddenly fit the kindred (or Wan-Kuei for that matter) within Fera cosmology in a most surprising manner indeed, vampires as the Gaia's detox/sin-eaters, while explaining the propensity to ping on Sense Wyrm and yet having no ties whatsoever to banes, black spirals and other such stuff (or why animals offer so little vitae - almost no bane-attracting behaviour contamination on them).<br /> <br /> <br /> Going a little further into this slippery slope, Genesis 4:10 And the LORD said, \u201cWhat have you done? The voice of your brother\u2019s blood is crying to me from the ground&quot; brings whole new levels of meaning (and cosmological crossover) to the equation.<br /> <br /> Is the Stone That Weeps Blood truly Zillah, an aspect of Gaia or a bit of both even?<br /> <br /> In some takes of Setite myth, the Founder claimed to have never been embraced, but to have drank the water of the river Du'at, that he equated with vitae.<br /> Exodus 17: 6 has Moses striking a rock and water springing from it for all of his community to drink.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 03-08-2023, 11:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T03:40:25-07:00","id":1485182,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus#post1477060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Narlthus was introduced very early in the game in the 1e sourcebook Rite of Passage. It was heavily tied to North America as the Wendigo Theurge She-Cries-Ice was the Garou that bound the bane (IMO she should be a Legendry Garou and awesome Ancestor spirit). Nevertheless, Narlthus was mentioned as a Talon of the Wyrm that meant it was intrinsically involved with the myths of the entire Garou Nation, not just the Three Brothers. In my own chronicles, I have the Eurasian tribes recognize Narlthus as a major threat, but ignorant of how it was defeated until they finally made contact with the Three Brothers (some 10,000 to 12,000 years later!).<br /> <br /> I am working on creating various &quot;factions&quot; of the Wyrm to provide the game more depth in my chronicles. So I associate corrupt winter spirits with Narltus and the false Wendigo (of Ways of the Wolf sourcebook) and other bane spirits that make sense.<br /> <br /> As inspiration, I use a lot of the Ithaqua mythos of August Derleth (prominent in the Call of Cthulhu RPG) and similar media. I want the spirit to be more mysterious to Eurasian Garou than the Three Brothers, but I make some reference to it to Fimbul Winter and such so that certain Old World tribes understand the Bane in its own way.<br /> <br /> Interested in what other people have done with this Bane in their chronicles, or what potential they think the Bane has. While as presented in Rite of Passage, the Bane/Incarna is a revelation to modern Garou, I prefer to use it as an ongoing threat that just became more relevant with the events of Rite of Passage. What do you think?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Dang, wish I'd seen this sooner. I have ran Rite of Passage! I modified Nalthus slightly to make is be what initially corrupts Samuel Haight. Rite of Passage melds almost perfectly into Valkenburg Foundation. If you have the chronicle on hand, I'd say that'd be a good place to look. If you want a full arc, you'd run it Rite of Passage, Valkenburg Foundation, Under a Blood Red Moon, and once word of the skinner reaches the party, they decide to end it once and for all with Rage Across the Amazon. Another big perk of running these is it more or less runs your players through the full spectrum of WoD baddies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/448972/Hearthbound-Tribe-Supplement?affiliate_id=4046720\" target=\"_blank\">Hearthbound</a> and Project Director for <a href=\"https://www.tumblr.com/songoftrillium/729204622290550784/likes-dont-increase-visibility-to-others-please\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf: the Essentials</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27167-mundusartis","name":"mundusartis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27167_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T13:57:51-07:00","id":1485544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mundusartis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus?p=1485182#post1485182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Dang, wish I'd seen this sooner. I have ran Rite of Passage! I modified Nalthus slightly to make is be what initially corrupts Samuel Haight. Rite of Passage melds almost perfectly into Valkenburg Foundation. If you have the chronicle on hand, I'd say that'd be a good place to look. If you want a full arc, you'd run it Rite of Passage, Valkenburg Foundation, Under a Blood Red Moon, and once word of the skinner reaches the party, they decide to end it once and for all with Rage Across the Amazon. Another big perk of running these is it more or less runs your players through the full spectrum of WoD baddies.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can agree with you that RoP is a perfect start for VF (especially if PCs ran away in the first one, the first story of latter can be used as a second Rite of Passage) but I wouldn't use Narlthus as a corrupting factor for Haight. First of all Saskatchewan is too far away from Wyoming where Valkenburg Foundation is. Second I've always seen Haights as someone like Sergei Kravinoff aka Kraven the Hunter, a guy who likes challenge (not to mention his longing for a proof that he's as good as any Garou). It's harder to kill someone who is a darker, more fanatical version of you than someone who is just Veil.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}